---
// src/pages/guides/container-growing.astro
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const businessName = import.meta.env['PUBLIC_BUSINESS_NAME'] || 'Palm & Agave Nursery';
---

<Layout
  title="Growing Agaves and Palms in Containers — Plant Guides | Palm & Agave Nursery"
  description="How to choose containers, soil, and care for agaves and palms grown in pots. Container size, drainage, watering, and long-term care for Florida patios, entrances, and movable landscapes."
>
  <Header cartCount={0} />

  <div class="guide-page">
    <article class="guide-article container">
      <header class="guide-header">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol class="breadcrumb-list">
            <li><a href="/" class="breadcrumb-link">Home</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li><a href="/guides" class="breadcrumb-link">Guides</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li class="breadcrumb-current" aria-current="page">Container Growing</li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Container Growing</p>
        <h1 class="guide-title">Growing agaves and palms in containers</h1>
        <p class="guide-meta">Container growing is a practical way to enjoy agaves and palms on patios, at entrances, on porches, and in movable landscape features. The basics are simple, but containers change the rules for water, soil, and long-term care.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="guide-content">
        <p class="guide-intro">
          Container-grown agaves and palms offer flexibility. You can move a plant to a better light, rearrange a composition, or keep a specimen plant in a controlled setup. Containers also let you grow plants that might not be the best fit for the ground in your exact spot.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tradeoff is that containers dry out faster, hold nutrients differently, and limit root space. That means container plants need more attention than the same plant in the ground — not because they are fussier, but because the container environment is less forgiving.
        </p>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Choosing the right container</h2>
          <p>
            The container matters as much as the plant. The right pot supports the plant, drains properly, and is large enough to be stable without being so large that the soil stays wet for too long.
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li><strong>Drainage holes are essential.</strong> Without drainage, water accumulates and root problems follow.</li>
            <li><strong>Choose a size that fits the plant now, not one that is much too large.</strong> A small agave in a very large container holds excess moisture and is harder to manage.</li>
            <li><strong>Give a palm enough room for its root ball.</strong> Palms in containers need enough space for roots to function, but not an extreme excess of soil.</li>
            <li><strong>Consider weight and stability.</strong> A tall palm in a lightweight pot can tip. A heavy pot can be difficult to move.</li>
            <li><strong>Think about the look.</strong> Container material, color, and shape affect the design as much as the plant does.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Soil and drainage</h2>
          <p>
            Use a well-draining soil mix. For agaves especially, drainage is critical. For palms, good drainage is also important, though many palms are a bit more tolerant of moisture than agaves as long as the soil does not stay soggy.
          </p>
          <p>
            A practical container mix is one that drains well but still holds enough structure to support the root ball. Avoid heavy, water-retentive mixes. Do not use garden soil in a container — it compacts and drains poorly.
          </p>
          <p>
            For agaves, a mix leaner in organic matter is often better than a rich, moisture-retentive one. For palms, a slightly richer mix can be acceptable as long as drainage remains good.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Watering</h2>
          <p>
            Containers dry out faster than the ground, and watering needs change with the season, the plant, the pot size, and the weather. The goal is to water when the plant needs it without keeping the soil constantly wet.
          </p>
          <p>
            General guidance:
          </p>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li>Check soil moisture regularly, especially in hot weather.</li>
            <li>Water thoroughly so moisture reaches the root zone, then let the soil dry appropriately before watering again.</li>
            <li>Agaves generally prefer a cycle of watering followed by a dry period.</li>
            <li>Palms may need more frequent watering than agaves in containers, but still not constant wetness.</li>
            <li>Reduce watering in cooler months when growth slows and evaporation decreases.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Overwatering is a common container problem. If a plant is struggling, the first question should often be whether it is being watered too much, not too little.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Light</h2>
          <p>
            Place containers where the plant can get the light it needs. A container plant can be moved, which is one of the advantages of container growing — but only if you actually move it when needed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most agaves do best in full sun or bright light. Most palms do best in full sun or bright partial shade, depending on species. If a plant is listed as tolerating partial shade, remember that "tolerates" is not the same as "prefers."
          </p>
          <p>
            Rotate containers occasionally if the plant starts leaning toward light. This is especially helpful for palms, which can develop a noticeable lean if light comes predominantly from one side.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Feeding</h2>
          <p>
            Container plants need some feeding because frequent watering leaches nutrients. At the same time, overfeeding can cause problems, especially for agaves, which generally prefer leaner conditions.
          </p>
          <p>
            Use a balanced approach: feed lightly and regularly rather than heavily and rarely. Follow product directions and adjust for the plant. Palms may benefit from nutrients commonly associated with palm health, but more fertilizer is not automatically better.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Repotting and root space</h2>
          <p>
            Repot a container plant when it has outgrown its pot — when roots fill the container, when growth slows because of root restriction, or when the plant becomes unstable. Do not repot on a rigid schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            When repotting:
          </p>
          <ol class="guide-list guide-list--ordered">
            <li>Choose a container that is one size larger, not dramatically larger.</li>
            <li>Use a fresh, well-draining mix.</li>
            <li>Place the plant at the same depth it was previously growing.</li>
            <li>Water in well after repotting.</li>
            <li>Avoid repotting during extreme heat or stress if you can wait.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Agaves often prefer to be somewhat root-bound, so do not rush to repot them. Palms may need more room as they grow, especially if they are actively putting on height.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Seasonality and movement</h2>
          <p>
            One of the advantages of containers is mobility. If a cold snap is forecast and the plant is cold-sensitive, you can move it to a more protected spot. If summer sun is harsher than expected, you can shift a plant to a spot with more afternoon shade.
          </p>
          <p>
            This flexibility is useful, but it only helps if you pay attention. Watch the weather, watch the plant, and use the container's mobility intentionally.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section">
          <h2 class="guide-section-heading">Common mistakes</h2>
          <ul class="guide-list">
            <li><strong>Too large a container.</strong> Excess soil stays wet too long and causes problems.</li>
            <li><strong>No drainage.</strong> A container without drainage is a risk for almost any plant.</li>
            <li><strong>Overwatering.</strong> More common than underwatering in containers, especially with good intentions.</li>
            <li><strong>Heavy garden soil in pots.</strong> Compacts, drains poorly, and creates root problems.</li>
            <li><strong>Ignoring the plant's light needs.</strong> A container plant still needs the right light, and moving it later is not always enough if it has already struggled.</li>
            <li><strong>Overfeeding.</strong> More fertilizer is not better, especially for agaves.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section guide-section--cta">
          <p class="guide-cta-text">
            Ready to start container growing? Browse our agave and palm catalogs and choose plants that fit the container size and the light your space can provide.
          </p>
          <div class="guide-cta-links">
            <a href="/shop/agaves?size=10" class="btn btn-primary">Compact Agaves for Containers</a>
            <a href="/shop/palms" class="btn btn-outline">Palms for Containers</a>
            <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-outline">Ask a Question</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .guide-page { padding: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-16); }
  .guide-article { max-width: 760px; }

  .breadcrumb { margin-bottom: var(--space-8); padding-bottom: var(--space-5); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .breadcrumb-list { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  .breadcrumb-link { color: var(--color-text-secondary); text-decoration: none; transition: color var(--transition-fast); }
  .breadcrumb-link:hover, .breadcrumb-link:focus-visible { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .breadcrumb-current { color: var(--color-text-primary); font-weight: var(--font-medium); }

  .guide-eyebrow { font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: var(--font-semibold); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.12em; color: var(--color-sage); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .guide-title { font-size: clamp(var(--text-2xl), 4vw, var(--text-4xl)); color: var(--color-green-deep); line-height: 1.15; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .guide-meta { font-size: var(--text-base); color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 600px; line-height: 1.6; }

  .guide-content { margin-top: var(--space-10); }
  .guide-intro { font-size: var(--text-lg); line-height: 1.75; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }

  .guide-section { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .guide-section-heading { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-xl); color: var(--color-green-deep); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .guide-section p { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.75; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }

  .guide-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .guide-list li { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-secondary); padding-left: var(--space-5); position: relative; }
  .guide-list li::before { content: '—'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-sage); }

  .guide-list--ordered { counter-reset: guide-ordered; list-style: none; }
  .guide-list--ordered li { counter-increment: guide-ordered; }
  .guide-list--ordered li::before { content: counter(guide-ordered) '.'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-green-deep); font-weight: var(--font-semibold); }

  .guide-section--cta { padding: var(--space-8); background-color: rgba(41,79,59,0.04); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); }
  .guide-cta-text { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .guide-cta-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-3); }
</style>
